# money_sums:
# CSES 1745, Money Sums (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1745), in our own
# words: given the values of some coins, the sums of money that some nonempty
# set of the coins makes, each sum listed once, in increasing order. The input is
# a Scott list (corpora/library/prelude/list) of positive binary naturals
# (corpora/library/binary/natural) and the answer a Scott list of binary naturals.
# Part of the CSES port's third batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: the list of the sums of all nonempty
# subsets of the coins is built (the subsets of a coin followed by the others are
# the subsets of the others, the coin alone, and the coin added to each subset of
# the others, so that a sum appears once for each subset that makes it), and
# its sums are inserted one by one into a list that is kept increasing and
# without repeats. Its reading is exponential in the number of coins even under
# tabling, since the 2^n - 1 subset sums are built; the replacement the family asks
# for is the reachability table of subset sums, reachable(s) = reachable(s) or
# reachable(s - coin) for each coin with the sums s descending, about n times the total of the coins
# steps, on fixed-width integers.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal. It β-reduces
# to V, the Scott list of the canonical binary numerals of the answer: every
# recursion ends, since nonempty_sums consumes the finite list of coins and
# every other recursion consumes a finite list; the branches that a Scott boolean
# discards are never read. The program calls no subtraction. So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable
# equations for the contextual preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons append map _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ _ merge_binary_plus _ merge_binary_equality
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "order")
λ binary_less_than _ _
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ. fixpoint
    λ self coins
    . coins nil
      λ coin others
      . ( λ without
          . cons coin λ. append without λ. map (λsum. merge_binary_plus sum coin) without
        ) λ. self others
  )
λ nonempty_sums
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ. fixpoint
    λ self value sorted
    . sorted (cons value nil)
      λ head tail
      . binary_less_than value head (cons value sorted) (merge_binary_equality value head sorted (cons head λ. self value tail))
  )
λ insert
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ. fixpoint
    λ self sums accumulated
    . sums accumulated
      λ sum others
      . self others (insert sum accumulated)
  )
λ sorted_distinct
. (λtuple. tuple λcoins. sorted_distinct (nonempty_sums coins) nil)
λ money_sums
. export money_sums
